% === provenance: Tricco2015RapidReviews ===
% CLAIM: A rapid review is a knowledge synthesis in which steps of the
%   systematic review process are simplified or omitted to save time;
%   typical shortcuts are a limited search and screening by one person.
% FOUND-VIA: scholar search '"rapid review" AND ("single reviewer" OR
%   "single screening" OR shortcuts OR "streamlined") AND (bias OR accuracy
%   OR "missed studies" OR limitations)' -n mfa-method -p openalex (also
%   wos, scopus, ieee, arxiv with the same keywords), 2026-11-12.
% PICKED: a scoping review of 100 articles on rapid-review methods, open
%   access; states the working definition that later guidance reuses and
%   tabulates which shortcuts are taken in practice.
% QUOTE (Background): "Rapid reviews are a form of knowledge synthesis in
%   which components of the systematic review process are simplified or
%   omitted to produce information in a timely manner"
% QUOTE (Methods, Definition): "A formal definition for a rapid review
%   does not exist."
% VERIFIED: full text read (Europe PMC full-text XML of PMC4574114,
%   https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4574114/fullTextXML,
%   accessed 2026-11-12); the definition is the paper's stated working
%   definition.
% COUNTER: same session, query S1 ('"rapid review" AND "scoping review" AND
%   "systematic review" AND (typology OR "review types" OR definitions OR
%   methodologies)'): the definition is contested only in that no formal
%   one exists, which the source says itself; the claim is worded as "a
%   working definition".
% DATE: 2026-11-12
@article{Tricco2015RapidReviews,
  author = {Tricco, Andrea C. and Antony, Jesmin and Zarin, Wasifa and
            Strifler, Lisa and Ghassemi, Marco and Ivory, John and
